<?xml version="1.0" ?><section xml:id="chapter2_values">
  <title>Values</title>
  <p>
  A <term>value</term> is one of the fundamental things—like a letter or a number—that a program manipulates. The only values we have manipulated so far are text values we have been outputting, like <q>Hello, world.</q>. Text enclosed in quotation marks like this is called a <term>string literal</term>. Later, we will use string literals to create and initialize std::string objects.
 </p>
 
 <p>
  For example:
</p>

<program language="cpp">
  <input>
std::string greeting = "Hello";
  </input>
</program>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p><c>"Hello"</c> is a <term>string literal</term>.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><c>greeting</c> is an <c>std::string</c> object.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      The string literal <c>"Hello"</c> is used to initialize the
      <c>std::string</c> object <c>greeting</c>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
  <p>There many different kinds of values, called <term>types</term>. This includes integers and
    characters. An <term>integer</term> is a whole number like 1 or 17. You can output integer
    values the same way you output strings:</p>
  <program>
std::cout &lt;&lt; 17 &lt;&lt; std::endl ;
</program>
  <p>A <term>character</term> value is a letter or digit or punctuation mark enclosed in single
    quotes, like 'a' or '5'. You can output character values the same way:</p>
  <program>
std::cout &lt;&lt; '}' &lt;&lt; std::endl ;
</program>
  <p>This example outputs a single close squiggly-brace on a line by itself.</p>
  <p>It is easy to confuse different types of values, like <c>&quot;5&quot;</c>, <c>'5'</c>, and <c>5</c>, but if you pay
    attention to the punctuation, it should be clear that the first is a string, the second is a
    character and the third is an integer. The reason this distinction is important should become
    clear soon.</p>
  <exercise label="values_1">
    <statement>
      <p>A <fillin answer="char"/> value is a single letter, number, or punctuation enclosed in single quotes. </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive"/>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
  </exercise>
  <exercise label="values2-0">
    <statement>
      <p>Click on all integer VALUES. We will learn about the utility of <c>while</c> and lines 4-7 later.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback><p>
      Try again!
    </p></feedback>
    <areas>
      <cline><area correct="no">int main() {</area></cline>
      <cline>    <area correct="no">int x</area> = <area correct="yes">7</area>;</cline>
      <cline>    <area correct="no">char c</area> = <area correct="no">'8'</area>;</cline>
      <cline>    while (<area correct="no">x</area> &lt; <area correct="yes">10</area>) {</cline>
      <cline>        std::cout &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">c</area> &lt;&lt; std::endl ;</cline>
      <cline>        <area correct="no">x</area>++;</cline>
      <cline>    }</cline>
      <cline>    <area correct="no">c</area> = <area correct="no">'9'</area>;</cline>
      <cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;<area correct="no">It's the year</area> <area correct="no">3000</area>!&quot;;</cline>
      <cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">&quot;Just kidding, it's &quot;</area> &lt;&lt; <area correct="yes">2020</area> &lt;&lt; &quot;!&quot;;</cline>
      <cline>}</cline>
    </areas>
  </exercise>
  <exercise label="values2-1">
    <statement>
      <p>Click on all string VALUES.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback><p>
      Try again!
    </p></feedback>
    <areas>
      <cline>int main() {</cline>
      <cline>    char <area correct="no">init1</area> = <area correct="no">'<c>R</c>'</area>;</cline>
      <cline>   std::string <area correct="no">init2</area> = <area correct="yes">&quot;M&quot;</area>;</cline>
      <cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">init1</area> &lt;&lt; <area correct="yes">&quot;+&quot;</area> &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">init2</area> &lt;&lt; std::endl ;</cline>
      <cline>   std::string <area correct="no">init3</area> = <area correct="yes">&quot;R&quot;</area>;</cline>
      <cline>    char <area correct="no">init4</area> = '<area correct="no">P</area>';</cline>
      <cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">init3</area> &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">'+'</area> &lt;&lt; <area correct="no">init4</area> &lt;&lt; std::endl ;</cline>
      <cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; <area correct="yes">&quot;Carved their initials in a tree!&quot;</area>;</cline>
      <cline>}</cline>
    </areas>
  </exercise>
  <exercise label="values_3">
    <statement>
      <p>Match the value to its data type by dragging items on the left to the boxes on the right.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback><p>
      Try again!
    </p></feedback>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise order="1"><p><c>1</c></p></premise>
        <response>integer</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="2"><p><c>&quot;1&quot;</c></p></premise>
        <response>string</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="3"><p><c>'1'</c></p></premise>
        <response>character</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>
</section>
